                    QOI 0.1b1 - documentation
                    =========================

  QOI pictures for every Amiga: qoi.datatype shows them in every
  program that opens pictures through datatypes, and ToQOI turns
  other pictures into QOI files.

  QOI ("Quite OK Image", https://qoiformat.org) is a lossless
  picture format by Dominic Szablewski: full colour, transparency,
  and a design simple enough to decode many times faster than PNG,
  which suits a 68k well.

  Source, issues and newest version:
  https://github.com/creep-ltx/AmigaDatatypes  (folder qoi)


  1. REQUIREMENTS
  ---------------

  - AmigaOS 3.0 or newer (datatypes came with 3.0). Developed and
    tested on 3.2; also tested on 3.1 and 3.2.1.
  - Any CPU: both files are plain 68000 code. The datatype also
    has a loop for the 68020 and later, chosen when it runs.
  - Full colour needs picture.datatype 43 or later (OS 3.5, 3.9,
    3.2). On OS 3.0 and 3.1 pictures are shown in 256 colours.
  - ToQOI reads other pictures through their own datatypes: it
    converts what the Amiga can already open.


  2. INSTALLATION
  ---------------

  1. Copy Classes/DataTypes/qoi.datatype to SYS:Classes/DataTypes/
  2. Copy Devs/DataTypes/QOI to DEVS:DataTypes/
  3. Copy C/ToQOI to C:
  4. In a Shell:

         AddDataTypes REFRESH

  If another qoi.datatype was installed before, restart the Amiga
  instead: AddDataTypes REFRESH does not reload a descriptor the
  system already knows.

  To check: Version SYS:Classes/DataTypes/qoi.datatype and
  Version C:ToQOI both say 0.1b1.


  3. QOI.DATATYPE
  ---------------

  Once installed, a QOI picture opens like any other: double-click
  it on Workbench, load it in MultiView, preview it in a file
  manager, or load it in a paint program that reads pictures
  through datatypes. It is recognised by its first four bytes
  ("qoif"), so the name does not matter.

  What you get depends on the picture.datatype in your system:

    OS 3.2 (picture.datatype 47)
      Full colour with transparency. The pixels are decoded
      straight into picture.datatype's own buffer, with no copy.
      Semi-transparent pixels are drawn over the window
      background.

    OS 3.5 and 3.9 (picture.datatype 43 to 46)
      Full colour with transparency, handed over 32 KB of rows at
      a time.

    OS 3.0 and 3.1 (picture.datatype 39 to 42)
      256 colours. A picture with 256 colours or fewer keeps
      exactly its own colours. A bigger one gets the best 256
      (median cut) with Floyd-Steinberg dithering. Transparent
      parts are shown over Workbench's grey. The picture is
      decoded twice instead of being kept in full colour, to save
      memory.

  A picture that says it has transparency but has none (every
  pixel solid) is shown as a plain one.

  Speed. Opening a picture (the datatype's own part) on the real
  A1200 (PiStorm with Emu68, OS 3.2):

    Picture                     QOI      older QOI dt   PNG
    800x600 with transparency   26 ms    63 ms          231 ms
    768x512 photo               38 ms    109 ms         133 ms
    1152x858 photo              68 ms    243 ms         300 ms

  ("older QOI dt" is qoi-datatype 0.6 from Aminet; "PNG" is the
  same picture as PNG through OS 3.2's png.datatype.) The older QOI
  datatype does not work on OS 3.1 at all; it needs
  picture.datatype 43.

  Memory. The file is read 16 KB at a time and never held whole.

  Broken files. Every pixel of every picture is the same as the
  reference decoder's (qoi.h), also for damaged and cut-short
  files. Checked on 450 test pictures (whole, damaged, cut short)
  on the PC and on the Amiga, and fuzzed on the PC with about a
  million runs. A damaged file is refused or shown with garbage,
  never a crash.


  4. COMPARED WITH THE OLDER QOI DATATYPE
  ---------------------------------------

  Aminet has had a QOI datatype since 2022: qoi-datatype 0.6 by
  Damian Gaweda. This one is written fresh, not from its source.
  Tested side by side on the same pictures:

                         qoi-datatype 0.6     this qoi.datatype
    OS 3.0 / 3.1         does not open        256 colours,
                         (needs picture.      dithered
                         datatype 43)
    OS 3.2               full colour          full colour
    Transparency         dropped (black)      kept
    Speed (real A1200)   63 / 109 / 243 ms    26 / 38 / 68 ms
    Broken files         no crash             no crash, every
                                              pixel as the
                                              reference decoder
    Converter            -                    ToQOI
    Size                 9.5 KB               13.8 KB

  (Speed: the three pictures of the table in section 3.) Both show
  the colours of a QOI picture exactly. The older datatype's
  readme asks for picture.datatype 43, so on a stock OS 3.0 or 3.1
  it was not meant to work.


  5. TOQOI
  --------

  ToQOI turns pictures into QOI files. It reads them through
  datatypes, so it takes every format the Amiga has a picture
  datatype for: PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, WebP, ILBM and the rest.

  Template:

      ToQOI FROM/A/M,TO/K,FORCE/S,QUIET/S

  FROM    One or more pictures. Patterns work (#?.png). Drawers
          and .info files that a pattern finds are passed over.
  TO      Where to write. A drawer: each QOI goes there under its
          picture's name. A file name: only for a single picture.
          Without TO, each QOI goes beside its picture.
  FORCE   Replace a QOI that is already there. Without it, ToQOI
          leaves an existing file alone and says so.
  QUIET   Print only what went wrong.

  The QOI gets the picture's name with its ending replaced by
  .qoi: photo.png becomes photo.qoi.

  Examples:

      ToQOI photo.png
      ToQOI Work:Pics/#?.(png|jpg|gif) TO Work:QOI
      ToQOI logo.gif TO RAM:logo.qoi FORCE

  For each picture it prints a line:

      photo.png -> photo.qoi: 800x600 with transparency,
        244 KB -> 507 KB

  From Workbench: ToQOI needs a tool icon first (with Show All
  Files in its drawer, select it and pick Icons > Snapshot, or
  give it any tool icon you like). Then select one or more
  pictures, hold Shift and double-click ToQOI. Each QOI goes
  beside its picture, and a requester says how many were made and
  what went wrong, if anything.

  Colours and transparency:

  - On OS 3.5 and later ToQOI reads every picture in full colour.
    PNG transparency is kept exactly.
  - The transparent colour of GIF and ILBM pictures is kept
    exactly (those pixels become fully transparent).
  - A picture that only says it has transparency (every pixel
    solid) is saved as a plain one.
  - On OS 3.0 and 3.1 there are no full-colour datatypes. ToQOI
    reads the picture's own bitmap and palette instead, HAM6, HAM8
    and Extra-Halfbrite included. A picture whose datatype gives
    only full colour (no bitmap) is not readable there; ToQOI says
    "this kind of picture needs OS 3.5 or later".

  What comes out is what the Amiga decodes. Tested by comparing
  every pixel of the QOI with the original on a PC: PNG, BMP, GIF
  and ILBM (plain, transparent colour, Extra-Halfbrite) are exact.
  A HAM picture comes out as OS 3.2's own picture.datatype shows
  it; ToQOI's own HAM reading on 3.0/3.1 gives the same bytes.
  A JPEG comes out as the Amiga's JPEG datatype decodes it, which
  differs a little from other JPEG decoders.

  Every byte ToQOI writes is the same as the reference encoder's
  (qoi.h), checked on 1,500 test pictures.

  Memory. The picture is read a few rows at a time and the file is
  written 16 KB at a time, so neither is ever held whole. The
  memory the picture's own datatype needs to load it comes on top.

  Safety. If anything goes wrong while writing (disk full, a read
  error), the half-written file is deleted. Ctrl-C stops ToQOI
  between pictures and in the middle of one; the unfinished file
  is deleted too.

  Speed. On the real A1200 (PiStorm with Emu68, OS 3.2), reading
  the picture included: an 800x600 PNG with transparency about
  0.27 s, a 768x512 PNG about 0.22 s, a 768x512 JPEG about 0.08 s.

  Return codes: 0 every picture was made; 5 something was passed
  over (not a picture, a QOI already, an existing file without
  FORCE) or Ctrl-C stopped it; 10 a picture could not be read or
  written; 20 the command line was wrong.


  6. TESTED
  ---------

  - qoi.datatype: the official QOI test pictures read back through
    datatypes and compared with the originals, every pixel and the
    transparency: the same, on OS 3.2.1 in FS-UAE and on the real
    A1200. Shown in MultiView on 3.2.1, 3.1 and a 68000 A600 with
    3.1 (FS-UAE). Opening and closing loses no memory.
  - ToQOI: PNG, JPEG, BMP, GIF and ILBM (plain, HAM6, HAM8,
    Extra-Halfbrite, transparent colour) converted on OS 3.2.1, 3.1
    and a 68000 A600 with 3.1 (FS-UAE) and on the real A1200, and
    compared with the originals as described in section 5. Started
    from Workbench, stopped with Ctrl-C, run many times in a row:
    no memory lost.


  7. BUILDING
  -----------

  With bebbo's m68k-amigaos-gcc: make in the qoi folder gives
  build/qoi.datatype, build/QOI and build/ToQOI. make check tests
  the decoder and the encoder against the reference ones on the PC
  (tests/ref/qoi.h, MIT, Dominic Szablewski).


  8. HISTORY
  ----------

  0.1b1 (17.10.2026)
    The first version. qoi.datatype: a picture.datatype subclass,
    full colour with transparency on picture.datatype 43 and later
    (straight into its buffer on 47), 256 colours with median cut
    and dithering on 3.0/3.1, a 68020 loop in assembly chosen at
    run time. ToQOI: pictures to QOI through datatypes, full colour
    on 3.5+, bitmap and palette on 3.0/3.1 (HAM and Extra-Halfbrite
    included), patterns, a target drawer, FORCE, QUIET, and a
    Workbench start.


